---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';
import { getPracticeURL, withPracticeURL } from '../../lib/practice';
import axeEnvelope from '../../samples/axe-envelope.json.txt?raw';
import errorEnvelope from '../../samples/error-envelope.json.txt?raw';

const practiceURL = getPracticeURL(Astro);
const jqViolations = `a1 axe ${practiceURL} --json 2>/dev/null \\\n  | jq -r '.result.violations[] | "\\(.id) (\\(.impact)) \\(.nodes | length)"'`;
const toc = [
   { id: 'exit-codes', label: 'Exit codes' },
   { id: 'json', label: 'JSON output' },
   { id: 'jq', label: 'Read JSON with jq' },
   { id: 'phrase', label: 'One line from a screen reader' },
   { id: 'baseline', label: 'Baselines' },
   { id: 'stdin', label: 'Test HTML without a server' },
   { id: 'reports', label: 'Report files' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Script the CLI"
   summary="<p>Every <code>a1</code> command sets an exit code by the same rules and prints JSON with <code>--json</code>. A script can stop on the first and read the second.</p>"
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section>
      <h2 id="exit-codes">Exit codes</h2>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt>0</dt>
         <dd>The command ran and nothing it checked failed.</dd>
         <dt>1</dt>
         <dd>The command line could not be read: an unknown command or option, or a missing argument.</dd>
         <dt>2</dt>
         <dd>Usage error: an option value that is not allowed, or an <code>sr</code> command with no active session.</dd>
         <dt>3</dt>
         <dd>The machine is not ready: no browser, a screen reader that failed to start, a setup step missing under <code>a1 doctor --strict</code>.</dd>
         <dt>4</dt>
         <dd>A check failed: a violation from <code>a1 axe</code> or <code>a1 check</code>, or a failed <code>sr expect</code>, <code>sr goto</code>, <code>sr find</code>, <code>sr wait --for</code>, or <code>sr batch</code>.</dd>
         <dt>5</dt>
         <dd>An error a1 did not classify, such as a page that did not load.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p>
         Fail a build on 4. It means the command worked and found a problem. The exit code is
         all a shell script needs:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="check.sh"><code>{`#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -e\n\na1 axe http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --fail-on serious`}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>--fail-on</code> sets the lowest <a href="/guides/violations#impact">impact</a>
         that counts: <code>minor</code>, <code>moderate</code>, <code>serious</code>, or
         <code>critical</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="json">JSON output</h2>
      <p>
         <code>--json</code> prints one object. It has the same top-level fields for every
         command. The examples scan the docs' <a href="/guides/demo">practice page</a>, a
         demo page with known problems.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe {practiceURL} --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{withPracticeURL(axeEnvelope, practiceURL)}</code></pre>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>ok</code></dt>
         <dd>Whether the command ran. It stays <code>true</code> when a scan finds violations.</dd>
         <dt><code>result</code></dt>
         <dd>The command's data. For a scan, <code>result.verdict.passed</code> is the pass or fail decision, after <code>--fail-on</code> and <code>--baseline</code>.</dd>
         <dt><code>errors</code></dt>
         <dd>On failure: one or more entries with a <code>code</code>, a <code>message</code>, and sometimes <code>details</code>. There is no <code>result</code>.</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag show 9.9.9 --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{errorEnvelope.trimEnd()}</code></pre>
      <p>
         The JSON goes to standard output. A one-line error for people goes to standard
         error, so <code>2&gt;/dev/null</code> leaves clean JSON for a parser.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="jq">Read JSON with jq</h2>
      <pre><code>{jqViolations}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{'button-name (critical) 3\ncolor-contrast (serious) 4\nimage-alt (critical) 3'}</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>a1 check --json</code> also lists the commands to run next, one per failing
         rule.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`a1 check ${practiceURL} --json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.result.nextCommands[]'`}</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{`a1 wcag rule button-name\na1 wcag rule color-contrast\na1 wcag rule image-alt\na1 sr walk ${practiceURL}\n...`}</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="phrase">One line from a screen reader</h2>
      <p>
         <code>--phrase</code> on a command that moves the cursor prints the announcement
         alone.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual {practiceURL}
a1 sr next heading --phrase</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>heading, Team, level 1</code></pre>
      <p>
         To run several steps and checks in one command, see
         <a href="/guides/screen-reader#batch"><code>sr batch</code></a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="baseline">Baselines</h2>
      <p>
         A site with existing violations fails on the first run. A baseline records them, so
         later runs fail only on new ones. Write the file once and commit it.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --baseline a11y-baseline.json --update-baseline
git add a11y-baseline.json</code></pre>
      <p>Then run without <code>--update-baseline</code>.</p>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --baseline a11y-baseline.json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>{'  Verdict:    ✓ nothing to fix  10 findings accepted from the baseline'}</code></pre>
      <p>
         A finding is matched by its rule and its element's selector. Fix one, and the next
         <code>--update-baseline</code> removes it from the file. <code>a1 check</code> takes
         the same two options.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="stdin">Test HTML without a server</h2>
      <p>
         <code>axe</code>, <code>tree</code>, and <code>check</code> take a file path,
         <code>-</code> for HTML on standard input, or <code>--html</code> with inline markup.
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`a1 axe src/components/dialog.html\necho '<button></button>' | a1 axe -\na1 axe --html '<img src="logo.png">'`}</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section>
      <h2 id="reports">Report files</h2>
      <p>
         <code>a1 axe</code> writes two standard formats. <code>--out</code> names the file.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
         <dt><code>--format sarif</code></dt>
         <dd>SARIF 2.1.0, for tools such as GitHub code scanning.</dd>
         <dt><code>--format earl</code></dt>
         <dd><a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/EARL10-Schema/">EARL</a>, the W3C's format for test results, as JSON-LD. <code>a1 check</code> writes it too.</dd>
      </dl>
      <pre><code>a1 axe http://localhost:3000 --format sarif --out results.sarif</code></pre>
   </section>
</DocShell>
